 "Eight-bar rhythmic phrasing: spine beat on strong beats, bar-level A/B/A/C variation, and a last-bar D that is either a fill (adds syncopation and toms) or an empty (drops the kick or main rhythm) to set up the next section",
 "Turn a one-bar loop into an 8-bar phrase that tells the listener where the section boundaries are. Build a spine beat (kick on 1-2-3-4, snare/clap on the backbeat 2 and 4, closed hat on the offbeat 'ands'), then put lighter percussion on the weak 16ths ('e' and 'a'), mixing low-mid and high sounds there. Use bar-level variation: A = core bar, B = one small change (add, remove or swap one hit), back to A in bar 3, C = a slightly bigger change at bar 4, then A material again, and D in bar 8: either a fill (extra syncopation, snare hits on weak beats, extra eighth-note kicks, toms high to low) or an empty (remove most or all main rhythmic elements, e.g. drop the kick for the last bar) before the next downbeat. The bigger the coming change, the more anticipation the fill or empty should build. Optionally layer a polymeter (for example a 3-step pattern over a 4-beat grid) so loops shift against each other over time.",
